Boomaga printer setup on Arch Linux
==================================

The package installs the application, CUPS backend and PPD. Creating a print
queue is a separate, one-time administrative step. Package installation and
upgrades do not contact CUPS or change existing printer queues.

Recommended: open Boomaga from your application menu, then choose
Help -> Add Boomaga as virtual printer. Review the command and confirm it.
Boomaga opens a terminal for sudo authentication and displays the result.
This requires sudo access and a supported terminal (for example Konsole).
Installing sudo does not itself grant administrative access. The GUI command
always uses sudo; displaying it as a fallback does not remove that requirement.

Alternatively, run the same command directly in a terminal:

    sudo lpadmin -p Boomaga -E -v boomaga:/ \
        -P /usr/share/ppd/boomaga/boomaga.ppd -o printer-is-shared=false

If sudo is unavailable, run lpadmin from a root shell without the sudo prefix.
Only printer setup needs administrative privileges; run the Boomaga GUI as
your normal user. An already configured queue does not require sudo to use.

CUPS must be running. If it is not, start it with:

    sudo systemctl start cups.service

From a root shell, omit sudo here as well.

Verify setup:

    lpstat -v Boomaga
    printf 'Boomaga test\n' | lp -d Boomaga

The test should open Boomaga with the submitted document. If the queue exists
but the application does not open, investigate the CUPS backend/session
activation separately; reinstalling the package does not diagnose that issue.
